A||N||2||kornholio||setting the timing on V6||||1||
Z||000000||kornholio||10-22-2000||05:18 PM||||I think it's time to check/adjust the timing on my V6.  I have a Haynes manual and it looks simple enough.  Is that all there is to it, or is there more I should know?  OR should I just pay a shop to do it for me?<P>firewall side<BR>6--4--2<P>5--3--1<BR>trunk side<P>This is the correct layout for the sparkplugs, right?<BR>Any tips or tricks would be appreciated.<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||10-22-2000||06:46 PM||||Your cylinder ID-ing is correct.  Timing is easy enough that you'll want to do it yourself, if you can see the timing marks.  Or take it to somebody you trust.<P>My only "tips" are: 1)Connect terminals A & B on the ALDL, 2)Put a swatch of screaming orange paint on either side of the mark on the crank damper, so the mark is easy to see.  3)Make sure you mark the widest of the three slots on the damper. 4)Use the average of cylinders #1 and #4, like it says on the emissions sticker. <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-22-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||tgowens||10-22-2000||09:44 PM||||if all you are doing is changing the plugs and/or wires, just replace them one at a time so you don't get the firing order wrong. you should have compression up in the #1 plug and then check the position of the roter (should be around 2 o'clock position) - that is where #1 on the cap will be.........timing? I set it by ear -- hasn't failed me yet.......<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                          <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group||||reg||1
